---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TraceStrip from '../components/TraceStrip.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const readExample = `import { openEdf, blobSource, getSignal, readWindow, toPhysical } from 'edfcore';

const recording = await openEdf(blobSource(file));
const fp1 = getSignal(recording.header, 'Fp1');

const [chunk] = await readWindow(recording, {
  signalIndices: [fp1.index],
  startSeconds: 30,
  durationSeconds: 10,
});

const microvolts = toPhysical(fp1, chunk.signals[0].digital);`;

const diagnosticExample = `const recording = await openEdf(source);

for (const problem of recording.header.diagnostics) {
  console.log(problem.code, problem.byteOffset, problem.message);
}
// DEGENERATE_DIGITAL_RANGE 3400 signal 7 "EMG Chin" declares digitalMinimum ===
//   digitalMaximum === 0, so physical scale is undefined (division by zero). …`;

const gapExample = `// A discontinuous recording is a list of runs, never one continuous block.
const chunks = await readWindow(recording, {
  signalIndices: [0],
  startSeconds: 0,
  durationSeconds: 7200,
});

chunks.length;                    // one chunk per contiguous run
chunks[1].precededByGap;          // EdfGap — where the recorder stopped
`;

// Surveyed across every EDF package on npm in mid-2026. Figures drift; the shape does not.
const gaps = [
  ['Ships TypeScript types', 'No standalone reader does'],
  ['Random access to a time window', 'Nothing published — the file is loaded whole'],
  ['BDF / 24-bit samples', 'No published package can read them'],
  ['Discontinuous EDF+D', 'Rejected, or silently decoded as contiguous'],
  ['Typed, located errors', 'console.warn and null, or bare thrown strings'],
  ['Header validation', 'Essentially none'],
];
---

<Base
  title="edfcore"
  description="A typed, dependency-free TypeScript reader for EDF, EDF+, BDF and BDF+ biosignal files. Real random access, exact event times, and errors that name the byte."
  wide
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="page">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">EDF &middot; EDF+ &middot; BDF &middot; BDF+</p>
      <h1 class="reveal">Bytes in.<br />Microvolts out.</h1>
      <p class="lede reveal">
        edfcore reads EEG, sleep and other biosignal recordings in the browser and in Node.
        It gives you real random access into multi-hour files, event times that compare
        exactly, and — when a file is wrong — an error that names the byte.
      </p>

      <div class="actions reveal">
        <code class="install" id="install"
          ><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span> npm install edfcore</code
        >
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/quick-start">Quick start</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/demo">Open the inspector</a>
      </div>

      <p class="note reveal">
        Zero runtime dependencies. Works on a plain origin, with no build step required and no
        server involved.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <TraceStrip />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="page split">
      <div>
        <h2 class="reveal">Ten seconds out of twelve hours costs ten seconds of bytes.</h2>
        <p class="reveal measure">
          An EDF file stores every channel interleaved inside fixed-size data records, so a
          time window maps to a contiguous run of records. edfcore reads exactly that run.
          Opening a file reads the header and nothing else; it never scans, and it never
          materialises a recording you did not ask for.
        </p>
        <p class="reveal measure">
          Every chunk reports <code>byteLength</code> — the bytes actually read — because one
          channel still costs a whole record. Making that visible is more useful than hiding
          it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock code={readExample} lang="ts" caption="Reading a window in the browser" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-inset">
    <div class="page">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">Why it exists</p>
      <h2 class="reveal">The JavaScript ecosystem for EDF was, until now, mostly a warning.</h2>
      <p class="reveal measure lede-2">
        Python has excellent tooling for these formats. JavaScript did not, and the gap is not
        subtle. This is what a survey of every EDF package on npm turned up:
      </p>

      <div class="scroll-x reveal">
        <table class="gaps">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Capability</th><th scope="col">State of the art before edfcore</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              gaps.map(([capability, state]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{capability}</th>
                  <td>{state}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="reveal measure aside">
        The honest consequence is that people building EEG viewers do not reach for an EDF
        package at all — they hand-roll a parser and reproduce the same bugs. edfcore exists to
        end that, not to displace an incumbent. The strongest prior art,
        <a href="https://github.com/epicurrents/edf-reader" rel="noopener"
          >@epicurrents/edf-reader</a
        >, is genuinely good and worth using if you want a whole viewer framework.
        <a href="/docs/comparison">The full comparison is here.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="page split split-reverse">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">When the bytes are wrong</p>
        <h2 class="reveal">It refuses to invent data.</h2>
        <p class="reveal measure">
          One rule decides everything: if edfcore cannot continue without making something up,
          it throws; if it can continue truthfully, it records a diagnostic on the result.
          There is no third option, and nothing is ever written to the console.
        </p>
        <p class="reveal measure">
          When a header declares <code>digitalMinimum === digitalMaximum</code>, there is no
          scale to apply. The reference C implementation quietly substitutes a gain of 1 and
          hands back raw ADC counts labelled as microvolts. edfcore sets
          <code>signal.scale</code> to <code>undefined</code> instead, so the type system makes
          that outcome unrepresentable.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock
        code={diagnosticExample}
        lang="ts"
        caption="Diagnostics are values, not log lines"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-inset">
    <div class="page split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">Gaps are structural</p>
        <h2 class="reveal">A recording that stopped and restarted says so.</h2>
        <p class="reveal measure">
          Sleep and long-term-monitoring studies are routinely discontinuous. Most readers
          either reject those files or decode them as though the gaps were not there,
          producing a timeline that is silently wrong by hours.
        </p>
        <p class="reveal measure">
          <code>readWindow</code> always returns an array of chunks, one per contiguous run —
          for continuous files too. If two shapes existed, people would write against the easy
          one and it would break on the first real sleep study.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock code={gapExample} lang="ts" caption="EDF+D, handled honestly" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band closing">
    <div class="page">
      <h2 class="reveal">Try it on your own file.</h2>
      <p class="reveal measure">
        The inspector runs entirely in your browser. Nothing is uploaded, because there is no
        server to upload to — which is the same reason edfcore is worth using for clinical and
        research data in the first place.
      </p>
      <div class="actions reveal">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/demo">Open the inspector</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/docs/concepts">Read the concepts</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  const targets = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.reveal')];

  /*
   * One quiet gesture, used consistently: the hero strip carries the orchestrated moment and
   * everything else only needs to arrive without jumping.
   *
   * This is a CSS transition toggled by a class rather than a scripted animation. Text that
   * starts at opacity 0 must be guaranteed to reach opacity 1 — a Web Animation that never
   * progresses (a throttled or backgrounded tab) holds the element on its first keyframe and
   * outranks any inline style you try to rescue it with, which leaves the page blank. A
   * transition cannot fail that way, and reduced-motion is already handled globally.
   */
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      for (const entry of entries) {
        if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
        entry.target.classList.add('is-in');
        observer.unobserve(entry.target);
      }
    },
    { threshold: 0.12, rootMargin: '0px 0px -6% 0px' },
  );

  targets.forEach((element) => observer.observe(element));

  // Last resort: if the observer never fires, the page must still be readable.
  window.setTimeout(() => targets.forEach((el) => el.classList.add('is-in')), 2000);
</script>

<style>
  .reveal {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(14px);
    transition:
      opacity 550ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.24, 1),
      transform 550ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.24, 1);
  }

  .reveal.is-in {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
  }

  /* If scripting is unavailable the observer never runs, so nothing may depend on it. */
  @media (scripting: none) {
    .reveal {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  .hero {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 7rem) var(--s-8);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
  }

  .lede {
    margin-top: var(--s-5);
    max-width: 54ch;
    font-size: var(--t-md);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .lede-2 {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
  }

  .install {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    padding: 0.6rem var(--s-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-inset);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .prompt {
    color: var(--accent);
    user-select: none;
  }

  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.6rem var(--s-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 140ms ease, background 140ms ease, color 140ms ease;
  }

  .btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--accent);
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .btn-primary:hover {
    background: var(--accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--accent-hover);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: var(--s-5);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .band {
    padding-block: var(--s-8);
  }

  .band-inset {
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.05fr);
    gap: var(--s-7);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .split-reverse > :first-child {
    order: 2;
  }

  .split p {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .gaps {
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: var(--s-5);
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .gaps th,
  .gaps td {
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .gaps thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    border-bottom-color: var(--rule);
  }

  .gaps tbody th {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    width: 34%;
  }

  .gaps td {
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .aside {
    margin-top: var(--s-5);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .closing {
    text-align: left;
  }

  .closing p {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-5);
    }
    .split-reverse > :first-child {
      order: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
